WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:12.067
On October 29, 1929, the president of the United Cigar Company opened his office window, climbed onto the window ledge, and leaped several stories to his death.

2
00:00:12.068 --> 00:00:21.052
That morning, a share in his company had been worth $133; by the afternoon, one share was worth only $4.

3
00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:24.274
Many other companies' values dropped that day.

4
00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:29.665
A share in General Motors fell from $262 to $8.

5
00:00:29.666 --> 00:00:34.729
Westinghouse shares went from $286 to $100.

6
00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:42.145
Before the day was over, many who participated in the stock market learned their shares were suddenly worth nothing.

7
00:00:42.146 --> 00:00:46.336
One and a half million people lost over $14 billion.

8
00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:57.043
The president of the United Cigar Company was not the only one to take his life that day; a small number of other businessmen also committed suicide when stock prices fell.

9
00:00:57.044 --> 00:01:03.853
This day came to be known as Black Tuesday, and it was a day that changed millions of people's lives.

10
00:01:03.854 --> 00:01:10.088
Before the stock market crash, the 1920s was a wonderful time in American history.

11
00:01:10.089 --> 00:01:15.644
The economy was strong, companies were making money, and there were plenty of jobs.

12
00:01:15.645 --> 00:01:21.688
People flocked to nightclubs to drink cocktails and dance to a new form of music called jazz.

13
00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:27.355
Many people from humble beginnings became millionaires in "rags-to-riches" stories.

14
00:01:27.356 --> 00:01:37.014
"Diamond" Jim Brady was a millionaire, famous for wearing expensive diamond rings and tiepins, but his first job was as a bellboy in a hotel.

15
00:01:37.015 --> 00:01:47.160
The wealthiest man of the day, John D. Rockefeller, began his career as a door-to-door salesman and accumulated a fortune of $1.4 billion.

16
00:01:47.161 --> 00:01:54.707
As people became wealthier, many invested their money in companies by buying shares, also called stocks.

17
00:01:54.708 --> 00:01:59.485
A person who owns a share in a company owns a piece of that company.

18
00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:09.030
When the company makes a profit, the people who own its shares─the shareholders─are paid a portion of the profit; this payment is called a dividend.

19
00:02:09.031 --> 00:02:16.697
A person who invests money in stocks will usually earn more from dividends than they would if they saved their money in a bank.

20
00:02:16.698 --> 00:02:23.037
When people wish to buy or sell shares, they turn to a professional trader called a stockbroker.

21
00:02:23.038 --> 00:02:27.632
A stock market is a place where stockbrokers buy and sell shares.

22
00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:31.967
In the 1920s, the value of stocks kept rising.

23
00:02:31.968 --> 00:02:36.943
Why, then, in October 1929, did their value suddenly fall?

24
00:02:36.944 --> 00:02:41.072
The answer has to do with the way people buy and sell stocks.

25
00:02:41.073 --> 00:02:45.659
The price of a stock is usually based on the profits that a company earns.

26
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:53.004
If a company is earning large profits and paying high dividends to its shareholders, then its shares are more expensive.

27
00:02:53.005 --> 00:03:00.432
Stockbrokers and investors, however, have become very skilled at estimating how much profit a company is likely to earn.

28
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:08.046
As a result, the price of a share is more often based on what stockbrokers believe the company will earn in the future.

29
00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:11.046
But investors can become too optimistic.

30
00:03:11.047 --> 00:03:19.647
During a time like the 1920s, when businesses were earning higher and higher profits, shares traded at higher and higher prices.

31
00:03:19.648 --> 00:03:28.299
Soon, it became easier to make money by buying shares and then selling them at an increased price than by waiting for the shares to pay dividends.

32
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:34.069
When many people buy and sell on this basis, stock prices can rise quickly.

33
00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:40.073
In 1929, prices for shares on American stock markets were rising rapidly.

34
00:03:40.074 --> 00:03:47.626
For example, during the summer, Westinghouse shares jumped in price from $151 to $268.

35
00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:58.122
This was an increase of 77 percent, but there was no reason to believe that Westinghouse would become 77 percent more profitable in the next year.

36
00:03:58.123 --> 00:04:06.217
People were willing to pay more for the stock because they thought they would always be able to sell their shares at a higher price than they paid for them.

37
00:04:06.218 --> 00:04:15.314
Eventually, stock prices became so inflated that people realized that the companies would never be profitable enough to justify the prices.

38
00:04:15.315 --> 00:04:20.386
Those who owned shares began to worry and started to sell rather than buy.

39
00:04:20.387 --> 00:04:26.195
Very soon, there were more sellers than buyers, and the prices of shares suddenly fell.

40
00:04:26.196 --> 00:04:33.923
When people learned that prices were falling, many tried to sell their shares as fast as possible for any amount of money.

41
00:04:33.924 --> 00:04:39.319
This is called panic selling, and it drives the prices down even more quickly.

42
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:45.731
When panic selling began on October 29, 1929, the stock market crashed.

43
00:04:45.732 --> 00:04:52.691
People who had invested their life savings in the stock market saw the shares they owned become almost worthless.

44
00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:57.098
Many others, who had borrowed money to buy stocks, were even worse off.

45
00:04:57.099 --> 00:05:01.950
The shares they had bought were now worth very little, but they were still deep in debt.

46
00:05:01.951 --> 00:05:05.378
The crash also hurt people who had never bought shares.

47
00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:12.711
That's because banks had invested in stocks as well, and the drop in share prices meant that banks also lost money.

48
00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:18.489
Banks that had loaned money to shareholders suffered further losses when loans were not repaid.

49
00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:25.090
After the crash, a person who went to a bank to withdraw money might discover that the bank had gone bankrupt.

50
00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:32.852
By 1933, one in four banks in the United States had closed, and millions of people had lost their savings.

51
00:05:32.853 --> 00:05:38.802
People had very little money to spend, and as a result, businesses couldn't sell their products.

52
00:05:38.803 --> 00:05:47.471
During these first few years after the crash, 86,000 companies went out of business. Unemployment rose to 25 percent.

53
00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:50.018
The Great Depression was at its peak.

54
00:05:50.019 --> 00:05:57.249
The Great Depression was a time of poverty and economic decline that lasted until the end of the 1930s.

55
00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:01.241
Desperate for work, many men became migrant workers.

56
00:06:01.242 --> 00:06:05.456
They hopped onto freight trains and traveled the country looking for jobs.

57
00:06:05.457 --> 00:06:10.075
Many did not have enough money to buy food and began to starve.

58
00:06:10.076 --> 00:06:15.831
Men and women lined up in the streets, waiting for a free meal at charities called soup kitchens.

59
00:06:15.832 --> 00:06:18.212
People could not afford to pay rent.

60
00:06:18.213 --> 00:06:23.434
Those who had nowhere to stay lived in huts they built from scrap metal and cardboard.

61
00:06:23.435 --> 00:06:26.434
It was a time of suffering and despair.

62
00:06:26.435 --> 00:06:32.818
In 1932, American voters elected a new president, Franklin D. Roosevelt.

63
00:06:32.819 --> 00:06:37.032
His government began many large projects to help create jobs.

64
00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:41.638
Dams and bridges were constructed; schools and libraries were built.

65
00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:47.162
All over America, men were hired to pave roads, lay bricks, and pour concrete.

66
00:06:47.163 --> 00:06:54.512
Construction of the Grand Coulee Dam, for example, employed thousands of men from 1933 to 1941.

67
00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:57.917
When it was finished, it was the largest dam in the world.

68
00:06:57.918 --> 00:07:04.679
Roosevelt called his plan the New Deal, and it included many government programs that continue today.

69
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:10.609
Social security is one such program, one that provides retired people with a pension.

70
00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:23.217
Roosevelt's New Deal also included a program to insure bank deposits, a program to provide unemployment benefits, and, of course, a government agency to oversee trading on the stock market.

71
00:07:23.218 --> 00:07:29.994
No government program, however, did as much to end the Great Depression as the Second World War.

72
00:07:29.995 --> 00:07:37.995
After America entered the war in 1941, the government began spending $250 million a day on the war effort.

73
00:07:37.996 --> 00:07:45.154
By the time the war was over, unemployment had dropped to less than 2 percent and industrial output had doubled.

74
00:07:45.155 --> 00:07:48.147
The struggles of the Great Depression were over.

